nternal/cli/cmd_serve.go         |   2 +-\n internal/github/fake/fake.go      |   7 ++\n internal/gitx/export_test.go      |  32 +++++++\n internal/gitx/local.go            |  19 ++++\n internal/janitor/janitor.go       | 187 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++\n internal/janitor/janitor_test.go  | 178 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++\n internal/servecmd/janitor.go      |  31 +++++++\n internal/servecmd/loops_test.go   |   4 +-\n internal/servecmd/rebuild_test.go |   4 +-\n internal/servecmd/serve.go        |   5 +-\n 12 files changed, 465 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)\n\ndiff --git a/CLAUDE.md b/CLAUDE.md\nindex 1c50ca0..c1204f5 100644\n--- a/CLAUDE.md\n+++ b/CLAUDE.md\n@@ -63,6 +63,7 @@ internal/reaper/    the Reaper loop (failed-job detection, retention, :running w\n internal/limits/    the book of exhausted usage limits the pool consults (the reactive half; #63 adds readings and thresholds)\n internal/intake/    intake admission: Admit decides whether a human-filed issue may enter triage (pure; the triage loop calls it)\n internal/prgate/    reviews-passed-at-head decision shared by the reviewer's mark-ready and the merger: label rule, head Covers, reviewed heads, ParseWorkerBranch\n+internal/janitor/   the janitor loop: prunes .ard/<issue#>/ of issues closed over 90 days ago through the main committer; no slot\n internal/merger/    the merger loop: gates (reviews, checks from check runs and the rules API, protected-path approval), one update and one squash merge per tick; no slot\n internal/reviewfix/  review fix rounds: the review_round event, Rounds/RequestRound, and the loop that sends :changes PRs back to the worker and reruns reviewers\n internal/reviewer/  the reviewer loop: claims `ard:review:<role>` on ard's own PRs, pins the head, runs the agent on the diff, closes out through the shared Finisher (one PR review)\ndiff --git a/docs/proposal.md b/docs/proposal.md\nindex c2c4acb..f6ccbf6 100644\n--- a/docs/proposal.md\n+++ b/docs/proposal.md\n@@ -161,7 +161,7 @@ Short summary of what was done.\n \n **Why `.ard/` stays on `main` (decided, #1).** An orphan `ard-state` branch is ruled out: worker and reviewer logs ride on PR branches, and a PR cannot carry files from unrelated history. The single committer, the `[skip ci]` marker and the path guard answer the usual objections. `[skip ci]` applies to main-branch bot commits only; commits on a PR branch never carry it, so CI always runs on them.\n \n-**Directories outlive their issues as the audit trail.** A monthly janitor deletes `.ard/<issue#>/` for issues closed more than 90 days ago; git history keeps them.\n+**Directories outlive their issues as the audit trail.** The janitor loop (`internal/janitor`) wakes daily but works once per 30 days (the timestamp is in memory, so a restart costs one extra harmless pass). It lists `.ard/` on a fresh fetch of `main`, finds closed issues (not PRs) with a directory whose close date is more than 90 days old, re-fetches each to catch a reopen, skips any carrying `ard:keep` or `ard:hold`, and deletes the issue's files through the main committer as `ard(janitor:prune)`, in requests of at most 256 files. Only numbered issue directories are candidates, and the committer's path guard enforces it; git history keeps what was deleted. Pause stops it.\n \n ## Scanners\n \n@@ -260,7 +260,7 @@ Each triage role owns one `##` section of `plan.md` with a fixed heading (Requir\n \n An executor runs one agent job somewhere and reports back; the controller does not care where. Both executors implement the same five calls: `prepare`, `start`, `status`, `logs`, `cleanup`. Prepare also removes any `Job.Scrub` paths (the job's own role file, directly under `.ard/<issue#>/`) from the fresh worktree before the agent starts. Job IDs are ULIDs minted at claim time; they name the worktree and the logs and appear as the `Ard-Job` commit trailer. The executor captures the job's stdout separately from its combined log, because the adapter parses one and the committed log comes from the other. Scanner jobs get network egress for advisory databases; agent jobs get only their model credential.\n \n-Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod: it starts `ard serve --loops scanners,stale,reaper` ([#187](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/187)), so the triage, worker and reviewer loops never run there, even though `.ard/config.yaml` on `main` has agent roles. `--loops` takes a comma-separated list of loop names (scanners, stale, triage, workers, reviewers, reaper), defaults to every loop, and rejects an unknown name with exit 2. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.\n+Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `